, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.58}, "rating_margin": -14.6, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.71, "baseline_margin": -10.03, "analyst_adjustment": -1.97, "key_factors": ["District's R+10 partisan lean in an open seat", "Unanimous expert ratings, mostly Solid/Safe R", "Gober's large fundraising and $1.6M outside support vs. Rourk's modest $364K", "No public general-election polling"], "rationale": "The R+10 lean, a Safe R rating consensus, and Polymarket pricing near 9% for Democrats all point to a comfortable Republican hold, and Gober's fundraising and outside backing reinforce that. The margin sits modestly inside the rating-implied figure given the open seat and absence of polling.", "watch": "Any late polling or outside spending surge tied to the Musk-focused news cycle could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Michael McCaul (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michael McCaul's retirement leaves an open seat in a central Texas district with a Republican lean of about ten points. Chris Gober, a redistricting lawyer who won a crowded GOP primary, has raised $2.6 million and benefits from roughly $1.6 million in outside support, while Democrat Caitlin Rourk has raised about $364,000. Every major rating outlet calls the race Solid or Safe Republican, and Polymarket prices a Democratic win near 9 percent.\n\nWith no public general-election polls, the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and markets, all pointing the same direction. The seat remains a Republican hold, with modest uncertainty from the open-seat dynamic and a news cycle that has drawn national attention to the race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0486, "margin": -10.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0248, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0573, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0508, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "202